# The EC sends one extra PS/2 scancode alongside every HONOR hotkey press. It
# carries no meaning of its own, and atkbd logs "Unknown key pressed" for each
# one, which on this machine is most of the dmesg noise during normal use.
#
# Two things here are easy to get wrong.
#
# The scancode is written f8, not e078. udev applies these through the legacy
# EVIOCSKEYCODE, where the scancode is used directly as an index into atkbd's
# 512-entry keymap. atkbd in translated set 2 folds the e0 prefix into the high
# bit, so the index is 0x78 | 0x80 = 0xf8, which is exactly what the kernel
# prints. An e078 entry is 57464, out of range, and udev rejects it quietly:
#
#   event2: Failed to call EVIOCSKEYCODE with scan code 0xe078,
#           and key code 240: Invalid argument
#
# The property still shows up in `udevadm info`, so the rule looks like it is
# working while the keymap is untouched. Verified here by writing both forms
# through the same ioctl udev uses: e078 rejected, f8 accepted.
#
# And the match is per BOARD rather than every HONOR laptop, or even every unit
# of one product. f8 is a real key index; silencing it on a machine that uses it
# for something would break that key. HONOR ships one product code as several
# boards, so "observed on a ZQC-P" is not the same statement as "true of every
# ZQC-P".
#
# This is a template. patch/hotkeys/install.sh fills in @HONOR_DMI_MATCH@ with
# the vendor, product and board revision of the machine it is running on, and
# the fix is tier B, so it only runs at all on a board somebody has verified.
#
# To add yours: press the hotkeys, look for "Unknown key pressed ... code 0xNN"
# in dmesg, and record it on your board's page under docs/hardware/.

evdev:atkbd:dmi:bvn*:bvr*:bd*:@HONOR_DMI_MATCH@
 KEYBOARD_KEY_f8=unknown
